    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d480">

                <pb facs="0674" n="574" sameAs="frus:frus1934v02/pg574"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">251.11 Insull, Samuel/366: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Greece</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >MacVeagh</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Athens</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-03-16T14:00:00+02:00">March 16, 1934—2
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received March 16—9:35 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>33. The Foreign Minister informed me this morning that Insull left Piraeus
                        Wednesday<anchor n="35" xml:id="d480-f1"/> at 4 p.m. for Port Said in small
                    Greek freighter named <hi rendition="#italic">Maiotis</hi>, owner Symonyminos.
                    Voyage will take approximately 3 days and I have cabled particulars to Cairo in
                    case this information is correct. Meanwhile on the basis of your telegram 10,
                    February 6, 5 p.m.,<anchor n="36" xml:id="d480-f2"/> I have told the Foreign
                    Minister that my Government does not desire to impede his flight into Egypt.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">MacVeagh</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
